  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-compact-metric-wasserstein-one": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(X,d)$ be a compact metric space. Write $\\mathcal B(X)$ for the sigma-algebra generated by the open sets of its metric topology. A Borel probability measure on $X$ is a countably additive measure on $\\mathcal B(X)$ with total mass one. For Borel probability measures $\\mu,\\nu$ on $X$, let $\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)$ be the set of Borel probability measures $\\gamma$ on $X\\times X$, with its product topology and Borel sigma-algebra, such that for every $A\\in\\mathcal B(X)$,\n$$\\gamma(A\\times X)=\\mu(A),\\qquad \\gamma(X\\times A)=\\nu(A).$$\nDefine\n$$W_1^X(\\mu,\\nu):=\\inf_{\\gamma\\in\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)}\\int_{X\\times X}d(x,y)\\,d\\gamma(x,y)\\in[0,+\\infty],$$\nwhere the infimum is taken in the extended real line and $\\inf\\varnothing=+\\infty$. The integrand is nonnegative and Borel: the triangle inequality and symmetry give\n$$|d(x,y)-d(x',y')|\\le d(x,x')+d(y,y'),$$\nso $(x,y)\\mapsto d(x,y)$ is continuous for the product topology; nonnegativity follows from the metric axioms."
    ],
    "lem-compact-metric-space-has-finite-borel-net-quantization": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space and let $\\varepsilon>0$. Then there are an integer $m\\ge1$, distinct points $x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\in X$, a Borel partition $C_1,\\ldots,C_m$ of $X$, and a Borel map $Q:X\\to F:=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ such that $Q(x)=x_i$ for $x\\in C_i$ and $d(x,Q(x))<\\varepsilon$ for every $x\\in X$. The partition assigns each point to a nearest point of $F$, and ties are assigned to the least index."
    ],
    "lem-mcshane-extension-from-a-finite-subset": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(X,d)$ be a metric space, let $F=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}\\subseteq X$ be finite and nonempty with distinct ordered points, and let $f_1,\\ldots,f_m\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy $|f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)$ for every $i,j$. Define\n$$\\widetilde f(x):=\\min_{1\\le i\\le m}\\bigl(f_i+d(x,x_i)\\bigr)\\qquad(x\\in X).$$\nThen $\\widetilde f$ is 1-Lipschitz on $X$ and $\\widetilde f(x_i)=f_i$ for every $i$."
    ],
    "lem-compact-metric-space-is-second-countable-under-countable-choice": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice. If $(X,d)$ is a compact metric space, then its metric topology has an at-most-countable basis; equivalently, $X$ is second-countable."
    ],
    "lem-finite-metric-plan-lifts-to-a-borel-coupling": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(X,d)$ be a metric space that is compact or second-countable. Let $x_1,\\ldots,x_m$ be distinct points, let $C_1,\\ldots,C_m$ be a Borel partition of $X$, and let $\\varepsilon>0$ satisfy $d(x,x_i)<\\varepsilon$ for every $x\\in C_i$. Let $\\mu,\\nu$ be Borel probability measures on $X$, set $p_i=\\mu(C_i)$ and $q_j=\\nu(C_j)$, and let $\\pi=(\\pi_{ij})$ be a finite coupling of the probability vectors $p,q$. Then there is a Borel probability measure $\\gamma$ on $X\\times X$ with marginals $\\mu,\\nu$ such that\n$$\\int_{X\\times X}d(x,y)\\,d\\gamma(x,y)\\le\\sum_{i,j=1}^m\\pi_{ij}d(x_i,x_j)+2\\varepsilon.$$"
    ],
    "thm-kantorovich-rubinstein-duality-on-compact-second-countable-metric-spaces": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space whose metric topology has an at-most-countable basis. Let $\\mu,\\nu$ be countably additive Borel probability measures on $X$, and fix $x_0\\in X$. Let $\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)$ be the Borel probability measures $\\gamma$ on the product-topology space $X\\times X$ satisfying $\\gamma(A\\times X)=\\mu(A)$ and $\\gamma(X\\times A)=\\nu(A)$ for every Borel set $A\\subseteq X$, and define $W_1^X(\\mu,\\nu)=\\inf_{\\gamma\\in\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)}\\int_{X\\times X}d(x,y)\\,d\\gamma(x,y)$. Then $\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)$ is nonempty and\n$$W_1^X(\\mu,\\nu)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_X f\\,d\\mu-\\int_X f\\,d\\nu:\\ f:X\\to\\mathbb R,\\ f(x_0)=0,\\ |f(x)-f(y)|\\le d(x,y)\\text{ for all }x,y\\in X\\right\\}.$$"
    ],
    "thm-kantorovich-rubinstein-duality-on-compact-metric-spaces-under-countable-choice": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space, let $\\mu,\\nu$ be countably additive Borel probability measures on $X$, and fix $x_0\\in X$. Define $\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)$ to be the Borel probability measures $\\gamma$ on the product-topology space $X\\times X$ satisfying $\\gamma(A\\times X)=\\mu(A)$ and $\\gamma(X\\times A)=\\nu(A)$ for every Borel set $A\\subseteq X$, and define $W_1^X(\\mu,\\nu)=\\inf_{\\gamma\\in\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)}\\int_{X\\times X}d(x,y)\\,d\\gamma(x,y)$. Then $\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)$ is nonempty and\n$$W_1^X(\\mu,\\nu)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_X f\\,d\\mu-\\int_X f\\,d\\nu:\\ f:X\\to\\mathbb R,\\ f(x_0)=0,\\ |f(x)-f(y)|\\le d(x,y)\\text{ for all }x,y\\in X\\right\\}.$$"
    ],
    "lem-borel-section-product-on-compact-metric-spaces": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $X,Y$ be nonempty compact metric spaces, let $\\mu,\\nu$ be countably additive Borel probability measures on $X,Y$, and give $X\\times Y$ its product topology. For every Borel set $E\\subseteq X\\times Y$, its section $E_x:=\\{y\\in Y:(x,y)\\in E\\}$ is Borel in $Y$, and $x\\mapsto\\nu(E_x)$ is Borel measurable. The formula\n$$\\lambda(E):=\\int_X\\nu(E_x)\\,d\\mu(x)$$\ndefines a Borel probability measure on $X\\times Y$ satisfying\n$$\\lambda(A\\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B)$$\nfor every Borel $A\\subseteq X$ and $B\\subseteq Y$. In particular its coordinate marginals are $\\mu,\\nu$."
    ],
    "thm-kantorovich-rubinstein-duality-on-compact-metric-spaces": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space. Let $\\mu,\\nu$ be countably additive Borel probability measures on $X$, and fix $x_0\\in X$. Let $\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)$ be the Borel probability measures $\\gamma$ on the product-topology space $X\\times X$ satisfying $\\gamma(A\\times X)=\\mu(A)$ and $\\gamma(X\\times A)=\\nu(A)$ for every Borel set $A\\subseteq X$, and define $W_1^X(\\mu,\\nu)=\\inf_{\\gamma\\in\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)}\\int_{X\\times X}d(x,y)\\,d\\gamma(x,y)$. Then $\\Pi_X(\\mu,\\nu)$ is nonempty and\n$$W_1^X(\\mu,\\nu)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_X f\\,d\\mu-\\int_X f\\,d\\nu:\\ f:X\\to\\mathbb R,\\ f(x_0)=0,\\ |f(x)-f(y)|\\le d(x,y)\\text{ for all }x,y\\in X\\right\\}.$$"
    ],
